const KC=[
{reign:'edward',unit:'Unit 1 – Stability of the Monarchy',heading:'1.1 – Issues of Edward VI\'s age',
checklist:'Issues of Edward\'s age and Mary\'s gender',
hints:['minority','regency council','reformists','conservatives','dry stamp'],
facts:['Henry VIII issued his Third Act of Succession in 1543, confirming that if Edward died without heirs the throne would pass to Mary and then Elizabeth.','The religious and factional struggles of the latter years of Henry\'s reign left a divided council between the reformists and the conservatives.','When Edward became King in January 1547 there were serious concerns about his age — he was only nine. It was feared that a regent might seize power, that Edward could not control faction, and that the image of monarchy as a strong, divine power would be challenged by a child.','Henry, fearing the negative influence of a single regent, instructed in his will that a Regency Council of 16 members should be formed, balanced between reformists such as Seymour and conservatives led by Gardiner and Norfolk.','In the last year of Henry\'s life, Gardiner and Norfolk\'s fall from grace in 1546 meant the Council was no longer balanced — it was weighted in favour of the reformists.','Following Henry\'s death, Seymour and Paget conspired using the dry stamp and the "unfulfilled gifts" clause of Henry\'s will. Seymour was made Duke of Somerset, Lord Protector and predominant figure on the Regency Council.']},

{reign:'edward',unit:'Unit 1 – Stability of the Monarchy',heading:'1.2 – Edwardian faction and Somerset\'s fall',
checklist:'Faction under Somerset and Northumberland',
hints:['Anti-Somerset faction','general summons','Windsor Castle','Warwick','Lord President'],
facts:['Factions on the Council were initially defined by religion: reformists and conservatives. Key reformers included Somerset and John Dudley (Warwick); key conservatives included Thomas Wriothesley.','Somerset\'s arrogant and dominating leadership created a new Anti-Somerset faction — a combination of reformers and conservatives including Warwick, Wriothesley and Paget.','They feared Somerset\'s apparent sympathy for the commoners and enclosure — they believed this had encouraged the unrest of 1549.','Feeling threatened, Somerset issued a general summons on 5th October 1549 for all loyal subjects to defend the King and himself against the Council. This was seen as inciting revolt.','On the 6th Somerset retreated to Windsor Castle but was forced to surrender when Edward turned against him. Somerset was arrested.','Warwick (now Duke of Northumberland) became Lord President of the Council in 1550. He released Somerset but had him executed for treason in 1552 — the evidence suggests the charges were fabricated to remove him as a rival.']},

{reign:'edward',unit:'Unit 1 – Stability of the Monarchy',heading:'1.3 – The Devise for the Succession, 1553',
checklist:'The succession of 1553 (Lady Jane Grey)',
hints:['Devise for the Succession','Lady Jane Grey','East Anglia','Northumberland'],
facts:['In early 1553 Edward VI\'s health began to decline. Mary was a committed Catholic — Edward was concerned she would reverse his Protestant changes.','The Devise for the Succession named the future male children of Jane Grey as Edward\'s heir. When Edward\'s illness progressed rapidly, he crossed out "male heirs" and named Jane Grey directly.','Edward died on 6th July 1553. His death was kept quiet for 2 days whilst nobles were pressured to sign the Devise.','Mary acted quickly — she had a significant power base in East Anglia and proclaimed herself Queen. The Council proclaimed Jane on the 10th, after Mary\'s proclamation on the 9th.','Mary raised a large force. Northumberland\'s army deserted him on the way — Councillors who had been forced to support the Devise broke ranks.','Mary triumphed. Northumberland, Lady Jane Grey and Guildford Dudley were later executed.']},

{reign:'edward',unit:'Unit 2 – Religious Change',heading:'2.1 – Edward\'s religious changes',
checklist:'Edward VI\'s religious policies',
hints:['Cranmer','Book of Common Prayer','chantries','iconoclasm','42 Articles'],
facts:['Despite Henry\'s Break from Rome, Henry never truly embraced Protestantism. The 1539 Act of Six Articles confirmed Catholic practices including Transubstantiation.','Stage 1 — Destruction, 1547: Royal injunctions encouraged iconoclasm. Repeal of the Six Articles. Dissolution of the Chantries. Repeal of the Treason Act — no longer treason to preach dissenting views.','Stage 2 — Limbo, 1548–49: Cranmer published the First Book of Common Prayer — a compromise between old and new, deliberately vague on Holy Communion. The Act of Uniformity enforced it nationwide. This Prayer Book prompted the Western Rebellion.','Stage 3 — Formulation, 1550–53: Northumberland\'s rise increased the pace. A new ordinal replaced Catholic bishops. The Second Book of Common Prayer (1552) was explicitly Calvinist — Communion became purely symbolic. The 42 Articles were submitted to Parliament in 1552 but Edward\'s death meant they were never passed.','By 1553, de jure England was a Protestant country.']},

{reign:'edward',unit:'Unit 2 – Religious Change',heading:'2.2 – Were Edward\'s changes successful?',
checklist:'Extent of de facto change under Edward',
hints:['de jure','de facto','visitations','Hooper','popular opposition'],
facts:['Just because de jure England was Protestant does not mean de facto this was true nationally.','Edward\'s rule lasted six years — in an age when ideas spread slowly this was a small amount of time to upend 1,000 years of Catholic worship.','Bishop Hooper\'s visitations around Gloucester diocese found that of 311 clergymen, 171 were unable to repeat the Ten Commandments and 40 could not find the Lord\'s Prayer.','The repeated efforts to remove icons and the visitations prove the changes were hard to enforce.','Evidence from people\'s wills shows little support for the changes except in London, south-east and East Anglia — the areas closest to the centre of power.','The rapid restoration of Catholicism under Mary might suggest traditional religion still retained its popularity. There was open rebellion against Protestantism in the West Country.']},

{reign:'edward',unit:'Unit 3 – Rebellion and Unrest',heading:'3.1 – Socioeconomic unrest: the Great Price Rise',
checklist:'Inflation, poverty, enclosure, and their links to unrest',
hints:['Malthusian crisis','debasement','enclosure','commonwealth men','Vagrancy Act'],
facts:['Inflation accelerated in the 1540s — food prices had doubled since the start of the century.','Population rise (Malthusian Crisis): from 1525–1551 the population rose from 2.3 to 3 million. Agricultural productivity could not keep pace — food prices rose and a Malthusian crisis developed.','Debasement: Henry VIII began reducing the precious metal in coinage to fund wars. This reduced confidence and prices rose to compensate. Somerset continued debasement to fund the Scottish war.','Enclosure: gentry transformed land from agriculture to livestock farming for the wool market. This produced less food, increased unemployment, and controversially enclosed common land.','Commonwealth Men — writers like Robert Crowley and Thomas Lever — blamed the Great Price Rise on enclosure and greedy gentlemen, adding to complaints of rack renting and regrating.','The Vagrancy Act of 1547 condemned vagrants to two years slavery for the first offence, lifetime slavery for a second. Somerset commissioned John Hales to investigate enclosures in 1548.']},

{reign:'edward',unit:'Unit 3 – Rebellion and Unrest',heading:'3.2 – 1549 and Kett\'s Rebellion',
checklist:'Causes, events, and significance of Kett\'s Rebellion',
hints:['Mousehold Heath','Flowerdew','Warwick','Dussindale','16,000'],
facts:['1549 was a year of serious unrest across the country — described as the "year of the many-headed monster". Risings occurred in over half the counties of England.','The lack of a strong royal presence in the minority meant central government appeared weak and more susceptible to rebellion.','Causes of Kett\'s Rebellion: the unrest began when a mob attacked the enclosures of John Flowerdew. Flowerdew redirected the protest to Robert Kett. Rebels\' demands concerned enclosure and high rents. In Norwich, 6% owned 60% of the land.','Events: 16,000 rebels met in July 1549; they marched on Norwich and camped at Mousehold Heath; they then stormed Norwich. Kett ran a well-ordered camp — justice was administered to show they could maintain order.','Government sent 14,000 troops. Warwick came and massacred the rebels at Dussindale — 3,000 were killed; 49 leaders were executed for treason.','Reasons for failure: Warwick had 12,000 well-trained troops from the Scottish war; Kett abandoned his well-defended camp at Mousehold Heath to meet Warwick at Dussindale.']},

{reign:'edward',unit:'Unit 3 – Rebellion and Unrest',heading:'3.3 – 1549 and the Western Rebellion',
checklist:'Causes, events, and significance of the Western Rebellion',
hints:['Prayer Book Rebellion','William Body','Lord John Russell','Sampford Courtenay','3,000'],
facts:['Causes: a prelude was the murder of William Body in 1548 — he had been tasked to supervise iconoclasm in Cornwall which was extremely unpopular.','There was large opposition to the First Book of Common Prayer in 1549. Many wanted the return of old Latin services — partly because Cornishmen were more familiar with Latin than English.','The demands were almost entirely concerned with halting religious change. However evidence suggests early demands excluded by the clergy included demands about high taxes and rents. At Bodmin rebels chanted "Kill all the gentlemen!".','Events: rebels gathered at Sampford Courtenay; gentry were attacked at St Michael\'s Mount, Bodmin and Trematon Castle; rebels marched on Exeter and besieged the city. A government force of 8,000 led by Lord John Russell defeated the rebels. Over 3,000 were executed.','Failure: unlike Kett\'s Rebellion, the Western Rebellion had no clear leadership and different sections attacked different targets.','How threatening? 1549 saw the most widespread unrest of the Tudor period — over 25 counties saw significant unrest. However at no point did the rebels attempt to march on London, and their demands were written politely to Somerset.']},

// MARY I
{reign:'mary',unit:'Unit 1 – Stability of the Monarchy',heading:'1.3 – The Devise for the Succession, 1553',
checklist:'The succession of 1553 (Lady Jane Grey)',
hints:['Devise for the Succession','Lady Jane Grey','East Anglia','Northumberland'],
facts:['In early 1553 Edward VI\'s health began to decline. Mary was a committed Catholic — Edward was concerned she would reverse his Protestant changes.','The Devise for the Succession was written naming the future male children of Jane Grey as Edward\'s heir. When Edward\'s illness progressed, he crossed out "male heirs" and named Jane Grey directly.','Edward died on 6th July 1553. His death was kept quiet for 2 days whilst nobles were pressured to sign the Devise.','Mary acted quickly — she had a significant power base in East Anglia and proclaimed herself Queen. The Council proclaimed Jane on the 10th, after Mary\'s proclamation on the 9th.','Mary raised a large force. Northumberland\'s army deserted him on the way. Mary triumphed. Northumberland, Lady Jane Grey and Guildford Dudley were later executed.']},

{reign:'mary',unit:'Unit 1 – Stability of the Monarchy',heading:'1.4/1.5 – Mary\'s gender and Marian faction',
checklist:'Issues of Edward\'s age and Mary\'s gender; Faction under Mary',
hints:['John Knox','Philip of Spain','Gardiner','Paget','Cardinal Pole','inner council'],
facts:['Mary was the first Queen to rule England in her own right. Many believed female rulers could not control powerful men, lead soldiers or display the power a monarch needed.','John Knox wrote The First Blast of the Trumpet Against the Monstrous Regiment of Women (1558) comparing female rulers to "blind leading those who can see".','Two major suitors: Edward Courtenay (championed by Gardiner) and Philip of Spain (championed by Paget and Mary\'s personal choice). A marriage treaty carefully protected English interests — Philip had no independent power; England would not be entangled in Spain\'s wars; Philip had no claim after Mary\'s death.','Mary\'s Council was dominated by three figures: Stephen Gardiner (long-standing committed Catholic, cautious), William Paget (exceptional administrator), and Cardinal Pole (zealous, returned from exile 1554).','Casual councillors were excluded from key committees in 1554; an inner council was established in 1555. Philip\'s departure in 1555 and Gardiner\'s death allowed Paget to dominate.','Gardiner and Pole disputed the pace of restoration: Gardiner stressed caution, Pole favoured dramatic change backed by persecution. For the majority of Mary\'s reign the Council worked effectively.']},

{reign:'mary',unit:'Unit 2 – Religious Change',heading:'2.3 – Mary\'s religious changes',
checklist:'Mary I\'s religious policies',
hints:['1st Act of Repeal','heresy laws','ex-monastic land','Cardinal Pole','12 Decrees'],
facts:['Mary\'s religious aims: undo religious changes since 1529; restore Papal authority; secure Catholicism long-term by marrying and having children; ensure Catholic religion is abided by across the country.','Parliament in October 1553 refused to repeal the Act of Supremacy, suggesting anti-papal feeling. However it passed the 1st Act of Repeal which restored the religious situation of 1547.','Mary used the royal prerogative to get rid of the Second Act of Uniformity and restore the Catholic Mass. Royal Injunctions of 1554 restored traditional Catholic practices: Holy Days, processions and ceremonies.','In April 1554, MPs refused to restore the heresy laws until Mary gave assurances ex-monastic land would not be restored. In November she accepted — the heresy laws were then revived.','Over the next 3 years, 284 Protestants were burnt at the stake, including Archbishop Cranmer and Bishops Hooper, Ridley and Latimer.','The Second Act of Repeal (November 1554) repealed all religious legislation approved since 1529, protecting property rights of those who had bought church land since 1536.','1556 — the 12 Decrees intended to improve clerical discipline, banning absenteeism, pluralism and simony.']},

{reign:'mary',unit:'Unit 2 – Religious Change',heading:'2.4 – Were Mary\'s changes successful?',
checklist:'Extent of de facto change under Mary; Attitudes to Marian policies including persecution',
hints:['Marian exiles','284','Foxe','Gardiner','de facto'],
facts:['Mary was initially welcomed with great enthusiasm — bells were rung, parliament opened with a sung mass, Oxford chalices reappeared and altars and crosses were set up in churches.','During the early months of 1554 c.800 Protestants began to leave England for Germany and Switzerland (Marian exiles). They helped develop an underground Protestant propaganda movement, keeping Cranmer\'s Prayer Book and Foxe\'s Book of Martyrs in circulation.','284 people were burnt at the stake (51 were women). They mostly took place in the south-east, London, Canterbury and Colchester — Protestant strongholds.','Gardiner opposed the burnings, believing them to be counter-productive. His death in 1555 meant they continued under Pole\'s direction. Large numbers attended the burnings but there is not much evidence the burnings converted the audience.','To what extent was England Catholic by Mary\'s death in 1558? Edward\'s short reign meant much of the country, especially the North, had always remained Catholic. Mary\'s five-year reign was insufficient to enact lasting change. Over 800 clergy were deprived for failing to return to Catholic practices.','By not producing an heir, Catholicism did not triumph. The time it took Elizabeth to establish Protestantism suggests the Marian regime had been successful in restoring Catholic practice.']},

{reign:'mary',unit:'Unit 3 – Rebellion and Unrest',heading:'3.4 – Wyatt\'s Rebellion, 1554',
checklist:'Causes, events and significance of Wyatt\'s Rebellion',
hints:['cloth trade','Kent','Guildhall speech','Duke of Norfolk','xenophobia'],
facts:['Causes — Mary\'s marriage to Philip of Spain: concerns over a foreign marriage, patronage going to the Spanish, and England being dragged into Habsburg wars. The timing — immediately after Mary proposed the marriage in Parliament — supports this.','However xenophobia may have been a tool to rally mass support. The rebellion began in Kent — a more Protestant area with trading links to Protestant Northern Europe. Part of the plot was to replace Mary with Elizabeth to defend Protestantism.','Many leaders had lost positions at court due to their faith — an attempt to regain old power. Collapse of the cloth trade in the 1550s created unemployment in Kent.','Events: only Wyatt in Kent rose — he raised 3,000 men. A government force led by the Duke of Norfolk deserted and joined Wyatt\'s men, declaring "We are all Englishmen!". Wyatt reached the gates of London.','Mary gave an impassioned speech at the Guildhall which secured Londoners\' support — they refused to open the city gates. The rebellion was defeated.','Reasons for failure: discovery of the plot forced the rebels to rise in winter, meaning fewer peasants were willing to join. Mary\'s strength and leadership ensured defeat.']},

// ELIZABETH I
{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 4 – Elizabeth I and Religion',heading:'4.1 – The Elizabethan Religious Settlement, 1559',
checklist:'The Religious Situation in 1558; The Elizabethan Religious Settlement',
hints:['Act of Supremacy','Act of Uniformity','Supreme Governor','via media','recusancy'],
facts:['The Act of Supremacy (1559) re-established Elizabeth as Supreme Governor of the Church of England — not Supreme Head, a concession to those who thought a woman could not be head of the church.','The Act of Uniformity (1559) restored the 1552 Book of Common Prayer with modifications — the Black Rubric (denying transubstantiation) was removed, making Communion deliberately ambiguous.','The fine for recusancy was set at 1 shilling per Sunday — very low, suggesting Elizabeth was not trying to force Catholicism into the open.','Elizabeth adopted a via media (middle way) — deliberately ambiguous on doctrine to allow both Protestants and moderate Catholics to conform.','Almost all bishops refused to accept the settlement and were replaced by Protestant-inclined clergymen. Two Catholic bishops were arrested during the Parliament of 1559.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 4 – Elizabeth I and Religion',heading:'4.2 – The Puritan challenge',
checklist:'The identity and aims of the Puritans; Puritan threat from inside the church; Puritan threat from parliament/popular appeals/Separatism; Elizabeth\'s archbishops',
hints:['Vestiarian Controversy','Cartwright','Admonition','Grindal','prophesyings','Whitgift','Marprelate Tracts'],
facts:['Puritans wished to strip the Church of remaining Catholic elements including elaborate vestments, episcopacy (church hierarchy) and ceremonial practices.','The Vestiarian Controversy, 1566: Archbishop Parker ordered clergy to wear vestments. 37 London clergymen refused. Bullinger from Zürich advised Puritans to accept the compromise.','Thomas Cartwright (Cambridge, 1570) argued bishops should be abolished — he lost his chair. Field and Wilcox\'s Admonition to Parliament (1572) demanded radical reform. The Marprelate Tracts (1588) used satirical attacks on bishops but alienated moderates.','Edmund Grindal was placed under house arrest for refusing to clamp down on prophesyings — informal clergy meetings discussing scripture.','Archbishop Whitgift\'s Three Articles (1583) required clergy to subscribe to royal supremacy and the Prayer Book — Puritan clergy who refused were suspended.','Robert Browne led a separatist church movement in Norwich in 1581. William Strickland was suspended from Parliament (1571) for attempting to alter the Prayer Book.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 4 – Elizabeth I and Religion',heading:'4.3 – Catholic threat before 1568',
checklist:'Catholic threat before 1568; Roles of the Papacy, Spanish and French',
hints:['Philip of Spain','recusancy','northern Catholics','Marian exiles','Pius IV'],
facts:['Philip of Spain persuaded Pope Pius IV not to excommunicate Elizabeth in 1563 — Philip hoped to maintain good relations with England.','The initial Catholic threat was limited: most English Catholics were in the North and prepared to conform outwardly rather than risk rebellion. Roughly one third of English nobles were recusants.','The Marian exiles returned with more radical Protestant ideas, while moderate Catholics were given time to conform.','The Dutch Revolt from the 1560s dominated Spanish attention, limiting Philip\'s ability to threaten Elizabeth directly.','The main external threats came from France and Scotland — the Guise family supported Mary Queen of Scots\' claim. The Treaty of Edinburgh (1560) temporarily neutralised the Scottish threat.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 4 – Elizabeth I and Religion',heading:'4.4 – Mary Queen of Scots, Rising of the North, Plots',
checklist:'Mary Queen of Scots, Rising of the North, Plots; Government reaction to the Catholic threat; Problems facing the Catholics',
hints:['Ridolfi Plot','Babington Plot','Walsingham','Bond of Association','1569','1587'],
facts:['Mary Queen of Scots fled to England in 1568 after losing power in Scotland. Elizabeth imprisoned her for 19 years — she was a focal point for Catholic plots.','Rising of the North (1569): Catholic rebellion led by the Earls of Northumberland and Westmoreland. Elizabeth raised 14,000 soldiers; around 450 rebels were hanged. The rebellion failed.','Pope Pius V excommunicated Elizabeth in 1570 (Regnans in Excelsis) — all Catholics encouraged to remove her from power.','Ridolfi Plot (1571): plan to assassinate Elizabeth; Duke of Norfolk executed. Possessing the Papal Bull was made treasonable. Throckmorton Plot (1583): exposed by Walsingham\'s spy network; led to the Bond of Association (1584).','Babington Plot (1586): Walsingham obtained Mary\'s written approval of assassination plans. Mary was executed in February 1587.','Government response: converting someone to Catholicism was treason in 1581; sheltering a priest was treason in 1585; recusancy fines raised to £20/month in 1581. In total 133 seminary priests and Jesuits were executed.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 5 – Government and Parliament',heading:'5.1 – Key figures at Court and on the Privy Council',
checklist:'Key figures at Court and on the Privy Council; Elizabeth\'s use and management of faction',
hints:['Cecil','Walsingham','Leicester','Hatton','faction'],
facts:['William Cecil served as Secretary of State 1558–72, then Lord Burghley — Elizabeth\'s most trusted and longest-serving minister.','Walsingham became Secretary of State in 1573 and ran an elaborate spy network that uncovered the Catholic plots.','The Privy Council had 19 members at the start of Elizabeth\'s reign. Elizabeth managed factional rivalries deliberately — Leicester and the Howards (Norfolk/Sussex) wore different coloured ribbons to signal their opposition.','Christopher Hatton "danced his way to favour" and became Lord Chancellor despite no legal training.','Elizabeth deliberately balanced factions, never allowing one councillor to dominate as Northumberland had under Edward VI.','Walsingham and Leicester campaigned for intervention in the Dutch Revolt from 1575; Cecil and others counselled caution. Walsingham witnessed the St Bartholomew\'s Day Massacre in France in 1572.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 5 – Government and Parliament',heading:'5.2 – Elizabeth, Parliament and the succession question',
checklist:'Nature of parliament in the 16th century; Parliament\'s relationship with the queen; Attitudes of Elizabeth, Parliament, and the Privy Council to marriage; The role of gender; Impact of marriage and succession on domestic and foreign affairs',
hints:['prorogation','Wentworth','taxation','marriage','succession'],
facts:['Elizabeth called Parliament only 10 times in her reign. Parliament\'s chief function was to grant taxation — Elizabeth needed parliamentary approval for all extraordinary revenue.','Parliament repeatedly pressured Elizabeth to marry and settle the succession. In 1566 Elizabeth accepted 1/3 of the subsidy rather than discuss marriage — a deliberate compromise.','Peter Wentworth was imprisoned in the Tower (1576) for arguing for free speech in Parliament — Elizabeth defended her right to control parliamentary debate.','William Strickland was suspended from Parliament (1571) for attempting to introduce a bill to alter the Prayer Book.','The royal prerogative — matters by custom only the monarch could decide — included the succession and choice of husband. Parliament could vote on taxation and legislation but not these royal matters.','Elizabeth prorogued (suspended) Parliament to prevent bills she disliked from being passed.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 6 – Financial, Economic and Social Affairs',heading:'6.1 – Financial and economic situation in 1558',
checklist:'Financial and economic situation in 1558; Ordinary and Extraordinary income; Methods of raising finance; Parliamentary taxation',
hints:['£227,000','Gresham','recoinage','surplus','First Fruits and Tenths'],
facts:['Elizabeth inherited a debt of £227,000 in 1558, with 14% interest owed to the Antwerp Exchange.','Ordinary income: feudal revenues, First Fruits and Tenths (church taxes). Extraordinary income required Parliament: subsidies, Fifteenths and Tenths (tax on movable goods in the countryside and towns).','Sir Thomas Gresham founded the Royal Exchange in London, reducing dependence on Antwerp.','Recoinage was completed by 1561 — Elizabeth replaced the debased coinage left by Henry VIII and Edward VI, stabilising the currency.','Elizabeth paid off her debts by 1576 and had accumulated a surplus of £300,000 by 1584.','The Book of Orders in 1586 banned hoarding of grain. Elizabeth encouraged Wednesday and Friday as fish days to support the fishing industry and naval skills.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 6 – Financial, Economic and Social Affairs',heading:'6.2 – Development of overseas trade',
checklist:'Development of overseas trade; Purveyance and monopolies; Impact of war on finances',
hints:['Muscovy Company','Levant Company','Drake','Hawkins','East India Company'],
facts:['At Elizabeth\'s accession, cloth accounted for 75% of exports and the Hanseatic League dominated much of English trade.','The Muscovy Company (founded under Mary I) traded with Russia. The Levant Company (1581) operated in the Eastern Mediterranean. The East India Company was founded in 1600.','John Hawkins began the transatlantic slave trade in 1562.','Francis Drake\'s circumnavigation (1577–80) seized £400,000 of Spanish treasure. Elizabeth knighted him on the Golden Hind.','Two attempts to colonise Roanoke island (Virginia) under Walter Raleigh (1585, 1587) both failed.','The Treaty of Nonsuch (1585) obligated Elizabeth to spend £126,000 a year on the Dutch Revolt. Elizabeth spent £4.5 million on the war after 1585 — consuming her surplus.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 6 – Financial, Economic and Social Affairs',heading:'6.3 – Changing attitudes and legislation to the poor',
checklist:'Changing attitudes and legislation to the poor',
hints:['vagrants','Houses of Correction','Statute of Artificers','Elizabethan Poor Law','1601'],
facts:['The Great Price Rise created high levels of poverty and vagrancy.','The Statute of Artificers (1563): tied young people into 7-year apprenticeships and regulated wages.','The building in London that became the pre-cursor to the House of Correction was Bridewell. Norwich innovated the use of a compulsory levy for the poor.','Houses of Correction were ordered to be built across England in 1576 for the able-bodied poor.','The Elizabethan Poor Law was passed in 1601, formalising a national framework: the deserving poor received relief; able-bodied vagrants faced punishment.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 7 – Later Years 1588–1603',heading:'7.1 – Defence of the royal prerogative in Parliament',
checklist:'Defence of the royal prerogative in Parliament',
hints:['monopolies','Wentworth','1597','1601','Golden Speech'],
facts:['Monopolies — exclusive trading licences granted to favourites — became a major grievance. MPs protested in 1597 and 1601.','Peter Wentworth wrote a 1591 pamphlet pushing Parliament\'s right to discuss the succession.','Elizabeth delivered her "Golden Speech" to Parliament in 1601, promising to review monopolies.','Parliament granted Elizabeth a double subsidy in 1589 and a triple subsidy in 1593 — reflecting the financial pressures of war.','Elizabeth defended the royal prerogative throughout — Parliament could vote on taxation but not on matters the monarch considered exclusively royal, such as the succession or choice of ministers.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 7 – Later Years 1588–1603',heading:'7.2 – Domestic effects of the war with Spain',
checklist:'Domestic effects of the war with Spain',
hints:['Spanish Armada','Tilbury','fireships','Gravelines','Treaty of Nonsuch'],
facts:['The Treaty of Nonsuch (1585) committed England to support the Dutch Revolt — England sent soldiers under the Earl of Leicester.','The Spanish Armada of 1588: Philip II launched 130 ships. The English used fireships at Calais to scatter the Armada. The Battle of Gravelines (August 1588) inflicted significant Spanish losses. Storms destroyed much of the remaining Armada off Scotland and Ireland.','Elizabeth gave her Tilbury speech: "I have the body of a weak and feeble woman, but I have the heart and stomach of a king."','The war continued until 1604. Elizabeth spent £4.5 million on war after 1585, consuming her surplus and creating debts.']},

{reign:'elizabeth',unit:'Unit 7 – Later Years 1588–1603',heading:'7.3 – Economic and social problems; faction and Essex\'s rebellion',
checklist:'Economic and social problems; The Irish rebellion; Faction and Essex\'s rebellion; Elizabeth\'s image and reputation',
hints:['Essex','Irish rebellion','monopolies','Tyrone','1601','Golden Speech'],
facts:['Robert Devereux, Earl of Essex became Elizabeth\'s favourite in the 1590s. He sacked Cadiz in 1596 but his campaign in Ireland (1599) against the Earl of Tyrone was a disaster.','Essex returned to England without permission, burst into Elizabeth\'s bedchamber, and was subsequently tried and executed for treason in 1601.','The Irish rebellion of Hugh O\'Neill, Earl of Tyrone, lasted from 1593–1603 and cost enormous sums to suppress.','Faction in the later years: Essex clashed with the Cecil faction. Essex\'s rebellion failed — Elizabeth signed his death warrant without hesitation.','Elizabeth\'s Golden Speech (1601) promised to review monopolies — widely praised as demonstrating her skill in managing Parliament and public opinion.','Historians debate Elizabeth\'s later years: she successfully managed war, the Irish rebellion and factional threats, but left behind debts, a succession crisis, and unresolved monopolies.']},
];

